WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
Adventures in the Magic Kingdom.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:07.800
This is your ticket to breathtaking adventures in the Magic Kingdom.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:31.160
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.280
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:39.380
Sean, how many parents use this as an excuse to say, you're going to Disney World?

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.200
Oh, I hope few.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.840
It's an evil thing to do, right?

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:52.760
Very evil. But, you know, what's funny is when I was a kid, we had these Disney sing-along tapes,

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.980
which I'm sure a lot of people grew up around.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:01.120
And it was basically just a collection of famous Disney songs with the actual scenes from the movies,

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.300
but it would have a little sing-along things, everybody could sing along.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.700
I don't know why I'm describing it.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:05.660
You all know what a sing-along is.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.840
The point being that they made a special one not related to scenes from movies, but just

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:16.160
from around the Magic Kingdom of Disney World in Orlando, Florida.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:24.860
And that was like, for me, from the age of three to the age of 13, when I finally got to go to Disney World,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.000
that was my way of going to Disney World, was putting on that tape, watching those songs,

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:36.080
and watching these other kids have fun in Disney World, because we didn't have YouTube back then.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:45.760
But I guess now, the three-year-old me is on YouTube watching, like, you know, some really snobby person go to hotels in Disney World and review them,

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.300
and say, like, this is what a five-star Disney World hotel looks like.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.260
Because that's, like, all the rage now on Disney World YouTube.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.940
My point being, you can't emulate the Magic Kingdom, Disney World, Disneyland.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:11.920
Like, the video game, it has too tall of a task in front of it to say that we're going to take the fun of Disney World and put it in your Nintendo Entertainment System.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:23.020
Well, I guess on one hand, I wouldn't know, because I've never been to Disney World, nor have I been to Disneyland.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.020
And I imagine they're similar.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:27.880
Maybe not.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.720
Again, wouldn't know.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:35.540
On the other hand, this is all I had, because this is one of the few NES games.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:41.940
In fact, this was the first NES game I ever played in my life.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:43.860
Whoa. Yeah.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.260
What a bombshell you just dropped on us.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.940
Yeah, and you know, when I say play, I mean I picked it up.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:58.420
Um, got annoyed with how it kept, like, crashing, because there was something wrong with the NES, I'm pretty sure.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.520
Um, and then never played it again.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.040
You needed a new 72-pin connector.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:20.100
Probably. Uh, but it's crazy how this game, from probably the under 45 minutes that I played it, uh, really did hold a place in my brain of just, like,

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.920
this is just what the NES is.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.500
And it did not paint a very pretty picture.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:33.020
Well, it's funny that you say that, because, in a way, right, Disney is huge with children.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.420
The NES is growing with children.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:41.560
It feels natural to have, like, more Disney games on the NES than what we currently have.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.620
Like, we don't have, really, we had, like, a Mickey Mouse game.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.900
We had, uh, the DuckTales game.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.400
I'm sure there's at least one other one I'm forgetting.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:04:01.860
But, like, this is the first one that really takes, like, Disney as a brand and says, like, hey, for 50 or 60 bucks, like, you can have that same experience as the Disney World kids, but at home.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.660
Uh, I, I, I am putting those words in their mouth, though.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:11.340
They aren't really saying this is a substitute for the Disney World experience.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:12.880
I'm projecting that.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.300
So let's, let's forget about that for a minute.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:25.260
And instead, just talk about the premise of the game, uh, that there are six keys that apparently open up the castle at the Magic Kingdom.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:36.780
And when I hear Magic Kingdom, I do think that is in Disney World in, um, Orlando, Florida, because that's one of the areas you can go to.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:43.060
You know, like, there's, there's Epcot, there's, um, the animal one, and then there's the Magic Kingdom.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.860
So I'm putting this in Orlando, not in Disneyland.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.480
I think they are, I think they are placing it in Orlando.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.240
Like, the, the whole thing, they call them attractions.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.800
It's not, like, a level or world.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:05:03.060
Like, that, that, you're walking around the park, and you're, like, you can see where people get refreshments.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.180
And one of them is, I think, even called, like, Space Mountain or whatever.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:12.980
So, yeah, it, it's definitely Disney World if you're, like, on mushrooms.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:15.400
Yeah, there you go.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:23.480
And so, uh, apparently I take six keys to open up this castle, and Goofy, I guess, left them all behind somewhere scattered throughout.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:26.680
So, you now have to, and also, like, they're not scattered out.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:28.360
They're actually just at the ride.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.780
Right, so, like, what's the deal?

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:36.740
Like, did he purposefully leave them there, and now it's up to you to have fun at Disney World by riding the rides in order to get the keys?

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.920
Like, was it a, was it a ruse all along?

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:44.220
I didn't, I didn't beat the game, so maybe that is the grand reveal that, like, we just wanted you to have a fun day at Disney World.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.080
And now that you did, you have all the keys, and you can go inside the castle.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.840
That said, I, I have a, I gripe right from the beginning of this game.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:06:05.120
As soon as you start, okay, you play as some kid that you don't get to design what they look like or whatever, but you do get to put some, uh, personal touch on by saying the name of the character that you want to play as.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:15.020
But all it says on screen is, please enter your name with an image of Goofy, uh, on it, like, with his hands out.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:16.300
Goofy telling you to do that.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:17.440
Please enter your name.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.200
And yes, you could read it that way, but you could also read it as, like, am I naming Goofy?

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.680
Like, is this some, is this some RPG?

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:31.320
Is that what you thought? I'm just saying, is this some kind of RPG where you know the real name of the character, but you can change it?

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:36.720
And so I know that that's Goofy, but maybe, like, I can change it so that I'm Mike this time.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:42.640
Uh, please enter your name does imply that you're, you know, you're gonna play as yourself.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:48.740
But with Goofy on the screen, it's not clear yet that you're not gonna be Goofy for the remainder of the game.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.240
You've already found out that he's the one who lost the keys.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:55.300
I'm gonna say real quick that this sounds like a U problem.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.300
Um, not so much a problem with the game.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:00.300
Interesting. All right.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.000
Uh, and for what it's worth, I didn't think that that was actually, uh, going to happen, of course.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.320
Oh, sure. It's a, you know, it's a confusing UI thing.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.240
And you know me, I don't, I, I'm very particular when it comes to the UIs.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:20.940
And so I think that they could have freed that up a little bit by just putting the kid that you're gonna be on the screen so that it's clear that's who you're naming.

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:22.380
I think we've been, that's just me.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:29.100
We've been ruined by how, like, beat you over the head with it that, like, naming yourself in Pokemon was.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:32.720
Like, they didn't, like, it wasn't Professor Oak saying, what's my name?

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.880
It was, they showed you this kid, right?

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:40.580
So. Well, that can't be me in this game anyway, because that kid is wearing a cowboy hat.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:42.840
And I've never been one to wear a cowboy hat.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:43.960
Yeah, it's really weird.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:47.360
Is that, is that a thing that kids used to just wear, like, around?

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:52.440
Like, I've seen movies where there's just little kids either dressed as sailors or dressed as cowboys.

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:58.420
Like, I guess you wouldn't know either, Mike, but it just seems like, was that a thing kids did?

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:05.940
I could understand if the kid that you play as in the game was getting dressed up, depending on, like, what ride he was in, right?

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.780
Like, you change costumes for the different areas or whatever.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:15.060
Like, that's fun. But just to be a kid who wears a cowboy hat, that's too bold of a kid, you know?

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.460
Yeah. Is that a Mickey Mouse thing?

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:21.920
Or maybe not bold of the kid, or maybe it's more cruel of the parents.

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:23.920
And, you know, it's funny, too.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:31.660
It's like, there's an easy opportunity here to just have him wear the Mickey Mouse ears that they want you to buy at the game park, right?

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:34.020
Like, just have it be like, oh, he's got the Mickey Mouse.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:36.460
Maybe they weren't around back then, but I'm pretty sure they were.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:49.980
Point being that it just feels like, it feels weird that you can't even just customize the character a little bit because he's such a, I feel like anybody who wears a cowboy hat has a lot of personality and therefore should just be an established character.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:51.700
I can't argue with that.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:56.220
The Magic Kingdom itself, though, looking kind of dull.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.140
Yeah, there's only ever, like, one other person in the park.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:01.920
And then they disappear.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:07.220
Yeah. And they're just there to make you feel bad about not knowing obscure Disney trivia.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.980
And, you know, if you do know that trivia, give you that other key.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.320
But otherwise, yeah, there's mean to you.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.140
I'll just tell you how crazy the trivia is, okay?

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:21.680
So first off, it's like, oh, you're a Disney fan?

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:23.600
What was the first Mickey Mouse cartoon ever made?

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:26.240
It's like, it doesn't have to start there, you know?

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:29.320
Like, I've only seen The Lion King and Aladdin.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:31.440
Don't, you know, don't jump on me.

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:33.900
Those movies also didn't come out yet.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:35.520
So let's focus on what would have came out.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:37.200
Sorry, I'm just a big Snow White fan.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:38.460
Don't know anything about Mickey Mouse.

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:43.280
But the question itself, what was the first Mickey Mouse cartoon ever made?

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:50.320
There's A, Steamboat Willie, B, Plane Crazy, C, Gallop and Gaucho.

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:55.580
And believe it or not, Steamboat Willie seems to be the obvious one.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:10:00.040
But it's not. It's actually Plane Crazy, the one that, like, Disney would never reference anymore today.

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:02.680
In Plane Crazy, I think Mickey's smoking a cigarette.

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:04.940
I don't even think I got that one.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:07.140
Maybe there's some randomness to this.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:08.040
It is randomized.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:13.760
Yes, it is. They have more questions, I think, than potential, like, answers or whatever.

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:17.360
That's actually infuriating. Because you would think that with these.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:19.520
Because I even got one that's just like a stat.

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:21.320
Like, sorry to cut you off.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:26.600
But it's just, how many Mickey Mouse cartoons are there?

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:29.820
And, like, this is a time before Wikipedia.

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:32.960
And I'm sure it's not even in any fucking encyclopedia.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.960
The actual number of Mickey Mouse cartoons there are.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.160
But it's like 169 or something.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:41.320
I don't know the actual number.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:43.380
But that was a question.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:52.660
And, like, I would have, before you said that, I'd be like, all right, well, you know, you'll just play the game enough times that you'll guess the two wrong ones if you're not right.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.700
And then eventually you'll get it.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:58.100
But, no, it's not even that.

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:00.700
It's like you have to know that?

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:08.280
Because, like, then it'll just give you a different question that you don't know process of elimination answers on.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.820
Sorry. Go ahead. Yeah, no, absolutely.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:17.040
Absolutely. And, you know, the trivia thing, too, though, it just needs to be reined in a little bit.

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:20.080
Because I do think it's too broad for Disney stuff.

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:24.140
Because this is the kind of thing that, again, I have some experience with as a child.

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:28.640
We had a, it kind of was a board game, but you didn't have to really play the board game.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.180
You could just grab the trivia cards and read the trivia questions.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:33.640
And there was two boxes.

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:38.580
There was a box of, like, 60 blue cards that were clearly for kids.

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:46.320
And then there was, like, 400 red cards that were, like, just the most insane things.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:57.900
It was like, what's the, you know, what's the cat's name in some random cartoon from 1937 that, you know, was shown maybe five times ever.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:01.640
And obviously there are people who are going to know that, right?

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:05.440
Just like there are people who are going to know every in and out of every NES game.

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:07.760
Maybe that'll be us by the time we're done with the podcast.

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:09.520
Probably not for sure.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:12.100
No, I don't even know what we played last week.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:16.940
Right. But my point being that, like, yes, anybody could answer those questions.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:40.780
But with trivia, especially when you're playing a video game for kids, like, you gotta rein it in a little bit and just say, like, if this is the same game where Space Mountain is just you pushing directions on the D-pad based on where the game tells you to go, that theoretically the question should be of the same kind of skill set that you can just kind of follow directions, right?

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:45.660
Yeah. And this is just sort of reiterating what you've just said.

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:56.540
But did you get the question, which of these non-Disney characters did not feature in Who Framed Roger Rabbit?

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:57.780
Did you get that question?

00:12:58.020 --> 00:12:59.420
I did not get that question.

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:00.900
It's like a double negative.

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:08.520
It's like, which of these didn't happen in, which of these don't exist and didn't happen anyway?

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:14.280
Right. I don't really understand why that's a question, why they wrote it that way.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:18.280
Like, there are so many other things you could have asked us about.

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:20.240
And, I mean, it ends up being Popeye.

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:29.860
But. And the trivia stuff doesn't have to be that bad if it wasn't the fact that a key is hidden behind.

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:31.900
Yeah, you can't finish the game unless you do that.

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:36.600
Right. And so, you know, for some people, it's not going to be a problem at all.

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:37.600
It's going to be very easy.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:39.120
They know Disney very well.

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:49.040
I don't think that, you know, probably the seven-year-old who picked up this game knows those kinds of questions, especially not the what's the first Mickey Mouse cartoon.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:52.100
All that to say, though, that's a small portion of the game.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:24.860
It's not going to be a problem. That's the real issue.

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:29.180
Like, you should be able to go into those shops or something, right?

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:35.040
You should be able to, like... They even have weird, like, icons that make it look like you can buy things there.

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:37.620
And maybe you can, and I just never figured out how.

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:45.880
You cannot. That's so strange, because otherwise it's just like, here's what looks like an ice cream cone, but it doesn't even look like a sign.

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:51.400
It just looks like there's an ice cream cone there, which would make sense if it was a gameplay thing, but it's not.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:53.840
So that's not important.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:57.700
Now, I can understand the park hasn't opened yet.

00:14:58.420 --> 00:14:59.800
I could buy that, right?

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:06.260
We got to find the six keys before the park opens or whatever, and who cares about these other kids that are here?

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:11.800
Because they're, you know, maybe they're insiders, too, or maybe they're part of the Mickey Mouse Club.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:18.660
But I can't get over the idea that something that is supposed to capture the excitement of Disney World,

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.680
a place where literally, like, every square inch is thought of and has, like,

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:26.780
oh, well, if that sign breaks, we have seven more of them in the basement just ready to go,

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:31.140
so this way nothing's ever looked broken or out of place and we can paint things in an instant.

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:35.160
To have your video game just look so bare makes me wonder, like,

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:39.820
how many people from Disney actually, like, approved and saw this thing before it came out?

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:41.880
And we won't know that answer.

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:44.780
So we're going to move on to the rides themselves.

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:48.120
The first ride I went on was Space Mountain.

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:51.980
I kind of just, I don't know if, like, because I went to Disney World on, like, Sean.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:59.120
I don't know if I just remembered whereabouts it might have been in the park or if it's just my brain always goes up and to the right.

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:00.340
But that's where it was.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:01.380
And so that's what I did.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:04.780
There's no order or requirement for this kind of thing.

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:08.260
So you can go to any of the rides and you can try and try again.

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:15.660
But I went to Space Mountain unaware that, like, each ride was going to be its own sort of minigame and different.

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:23.400
And I'll tell you, Space Mountain's probably not the one you want to go to first because while the concept is really easy,

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:26.720
you just follow the track that is laid out for you.

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:32.360
And I think it's supposed to be Mickey who's giving you the, like, the directions.

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:34.080
Yeah, he's telling you to do a barrel roll or something.

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:39.260
Right, right. So he's telling, you know, you'll see on the screen the arrow to the right and you're like,

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:40.720
oh, you have to press right on the D-pad.

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:44.980
And then you'll see an enemy ship appear and you'll be like, oh, I have to fire my laser.

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:46.780
And that's fine.

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:49.360
But you have to do that for a pretty long time.

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:52.920
You have to get to, like, sector F and you start in sector A.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:58.140
And then on top of that, it speeds up, like, the reaction times.

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:05.960
So it really becomes this thing where, like, you only have maybe a second to input the command by the end of it.

00:17:05.960 --> 00:17:09.120
And if you screw up three times, you're out of the ride.

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:16.160
